# name: Security patch level
# description: Set the OS and vendor security patch dates to the ones the TEE reports, so the properties and key attestation agree: Play Integrity Fork's SECURITY_PATCH if it's installed, else TEESimulator's patch levels for Google Play services, else the current month (OS YYYY-MM-01, vendor YYYY-MM-05, stepped back a month on the 1st-4th). Off by default: a patch date newer than this build's fingerprint is itself an inconsistency, so enable knowingly. 'reset' only rewrites a prop that already exists and differs.
# enabled: 0
# stage: post-fs-data
# (runs before zygote so the framework's Build.* value matches this property)

# {security_patch} and {vendor_patch} follow the patch levels the TEE reports,
# taken from the first of these that is installed and enabled:
#   1. Play Integrity Fork: SECURITY_PATCH (or its ro.build.version.security_patch
#      entry) from the config it loads, custom.pif.prop first. Both tokens get
#      this date, as Play Integrity Fork's own sync gives every TEE level it.
#   2. TEESimulator: the system and vendor patch levels of the profile in
#      /data/adb/teesim/config.json that lists com.google.android.gms, resolved
#      as TEESimulator does (today, YYYY-MM-05, an explicit date, or empty for
#      the harvested level). With no such profile, Play services is served by
#      the real TEE, so its harvested levels are used.
#   3. The calendar: {security_patch} -> current YYYY-MM-01, {vendor_patch} ->
#      current YYYY-MM-05, rolled back a month while the 5th is still ahead.
# A level TEESimulator sets to system_property follows this property, so it
# comes from the calendar too. The log says which source each date came from,
# and warns when TEESimulator disagrees with Play Integrity Fork.

reset  ro.build.version.security_patch  {security_patch}
reset  ro.vendor.build.security_patch   {vendor_patch}
